## Statement

Let $Q$ be any orientation of a finite ADE forest over $k=\mathbb F_q$,
with $\nu=\sqrt q>1$ and coefficient field $\mathbb Q(\nu)$.
Its Euler-twisted Hall algebra is generated by the vertex-simple classes.
Consequently its composition algebra equals the full positive Hall algebra,
and adjoining the declared Cartan torus gives the full extended Hall algebra.
No fixed-parameter quantum-group injectivity theorem or Hall polynomial
existence theorem is a premise.

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** The finite ADE forest, its finite-field representation category,
and the subobject-count Hall normalization.

[F1] Indecomposables $M_1,\ldots,M_m$ are indexed by positive roots,
with $\operatorname{End}M_i=k$, self-Ext zero and
$\operatorname{Ext}^1(M_i,M_j)=0$ for $i<j$
([[lem-finite-dynkin-quiver-reflection-classification]]).

[F2] Objects decompose uniquely into indecomposables by
[[thm-graded-krull-schmidt-for-finite-dimensional-graded-modules]],
applied to the path algebra and modules in degree zero.

[F3] The Hall coefficient counts subobjects in
$0\to N\to R\to M\to0$, multiplied by
$\nu^{\chi_0(M,N)}$. By [[lem-hall-euler-form-is-additive]],
$\chi_0(M,N)=\langle[M],[N]\rangle$ is bilinear on $K_0$; for
quiver representations the vertex-simple basis identifies these classes with
dimension vectors. The composition algebra is
the simple-generated subalgebra
([[def-euler-twisted-hall-algebra]],
[[def-extended-hall-algebra-and-composition-subalgebra]]).

## Proof

1.1 By [F1], every extension of $M_i^{\oplus a}$ by $M_j^{\oplus b}$ for $i\le j$ splits. Hence an ordered Hall product of such blocks is a nonzero scalar multiple of their direct sum: the only possible middle object is the direct sum, and at least its canonical summand inclusion contributes to the positive integer Hall count. Its Euler factor is a nonzero power of $\nu$. This proves that whenever the individual indecomposable classes in a sum have been generated, the class of that sum is generated. In particular $[S_i^{\oplus a}]$ is a nonzero scalar multiple of $[S_i]^a$, since there are no self-extensions at a loopless vertex. [F1, F2, F3, algebra]

2.1 Number the vertices topologically, with arrows from earlier to later vertices. For a dimension vector $d$, the product $[d_1S_1]\star\cdots\star[d_rS_r]$ has the same nonzero coefficient on every object of dimension $d$. Indeed it counts the unique descending filtration whose successive quotients are supported at these vertices: the subobject after each step is the entire representation on the remaining terminal vertex set and zero on the preceding vertices. This is a subrepresentation because arrows never go from a terminal vertex to an earlier one. There are no choices of subspaces within a vertex block. All Euler factors depend only on $d$, so their product is a common nonzero scalar. There are finitely many objects in the degree, since there are finitely many arrow matrices over $k$. [F3, step 1.1, algebra]

3.1 Induct on the total dimension of an indecomposable $M$. The one-dimensional case is a vertex simple. For larger dimension $d$, [F1] gives exactly one indecomposable of dimension $d$. Every other object of that degree is a direct sum of smaller-dimensional indecomposables by [F2]; its class is generated by induction and step 1.1. The simple word in step 2.1 is generated by step 1.1. Subtract all its other degree-$d$ classes and divide by its nonzero coefficient to isolate $[M]$. This proves generation of every indecomposable, and step 1.1 then proves generation of every object. The composition and extended conclusions are exactly their definitions in [F3]. Across distinct components the cross Euler form, Hom and Ext are zero, so the same argument covers every finite ADE forest, including the empty one. [F1, F2, F3, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra] ∎
